{"chunk_id": "4bb94e965b7c", "text": "This section (Bhagavad Gita, Chapter III) addresses Arjuna\u2019s confusion about whether knowledge and devotion are superior to action. Krishna explains that there are two spiritual paths\u2014knowledge and disciplined action\u2014but no one can truly avoid action. Instead, one should perform one\u2019s duty without attachment to results. Actions offered as sacrifice sustain the cosmic order, linking humans, gods, and nature in a reciprocal cycle. Great leaders must act responsibly because others imitate them. The wise act without ego, understanding that nature\u2019s qualities perform all works. Krishna identifies desire and anger as the great enemies that cloud knowledge. The teaching culminates in the principle of svadharma: performing one\u2019s own duty, even imperfectly, is better than doing another\u2019s well.\nKrishna teaches Arjuna that liberation comes through selfless action (karma\u2011yoga), performing one\u2019s duty without attachment and overcoming desire and anger.\nThemes: karma yoga, duty and detachment, cosmic order of sacrifice, leadership by example, desire as enemy", "vector": [-0.0024495229590684175, -0.013244225643575191, -0.009653129614889622, 0.00541300093755126, -0.016815554350614548, 0.015985319390892982, -0.020571378991007805, -0.014654307626187801, -0.009877161122858524, -0.00992328580468893, 0.012143834494054317, 0.005508543457835913, -0.0050308289937675, 0.0306264478713274, -0.0071558356285095215, -0.003185862209647894, 0.05129007622599602, -0.00666164793074131, 0.024577593430876732, 0.009817859157919884, -0.01768532395362854, -0.004688192158937454, 0.0051461393013596535, -0.016604701057076454, -0.007261262275278568, 0.010068247094750404, 0.016446560621261597, -0.033868320286273956, 0.007399634458124638, -0.027490004897117615, -0.01536593772470951, -0.009409330785274506, -0.006760485470294952, -0.023549683392047882, -0.04122183099389076, 0.00013044491061009467, -0.0050604804418981075, -0.011419